**assistant**:
> The error message is telling you that the file you're trying to read contains characters that the `utf-8` codec cannot decode. This is often due to the file being encoded with a different character set. To fix this, you should try reading the file with a different encoding. One common alternative is `ISO-8859-1` (also known as `latin-1`), which can handle byte values in the range 0-255 without raising errors.
> 
> Here is how you can do that:
> 
> ```python
> with open(filepath, 'r', encoding='ISO-8859-1') as file:
>     content = file.read()
> ```
> 
> Alternatively, if you're not sure about the encoding, you can use `errors='replace'` or `errors='ignore'` to handle undecodable bytes:
> 
> ```python
> with open(filepath, 'r', encoding='utf-8', errors='replace') as file:
>     content = file.read()
> ```
> 
> This will replace undecodable bytes with a placeholder character (`�`). Using `errors='ignore'` will simply skip over them silently. If you want to preserve the original bytes while gaining a clearer understanding of their content, you might consider using `chardet` or another encoding detection library to guess the encoding before reading the file.
